# A loop's word twin takes its int arguments as raw words. When a sum in the
# twin overflows before the body has written anything, the twin calls the
# general body with the arguments it was given, and the general body computes
# the bignum. `q` crosses 2^63 on the third step; the answer must be the
# exact sum, not a wrapped word.
fn climb 0 p
  p

fn climb k p
  q = p + 1
  climb (k - 1) (q + 0)

pub play = print (climb 4 9223372036854775805)
